  "final_assistant_text": "Both changes are in place:\n\n1. **`src/pipeline/steps.py`** — `normalize_step` now strips whitespace, then strips leading/trailing `/` characters, then strips any newly exposed whitespace, and lowercases. So:\n   - `normalize_step(\" /Deploy /\")` → `\" /Deploy /\"` → `\"Deploy \"` → `\"Deploy\"` → `\"deploy\"` ✔\n   - `normalize_step(\"a/b\")` → `\"a/b\"` (inner slash preserved) ✔\n\n2. **`src/pipeline/runner.py`** — `run_step` now imports and calls `normalize_step` instead of containing its own `strip().lower()` copy, so the rule exists in exactly one place.\n\nThe `README.md` and tests were left untouched.\n\nOne verification note: I attempted to run the suite with `PYTHONPATH=src python3 -B -m unittest discover -s tests -t tests`, but shell command execution is disabled in this environment (both `run_command` attempts were rejected by policy), so I could not execute the tests. Verification was instead done by careful inspection of the final file contents and reading the user-visible test file (`tests/test_pipeline.py`), which imports `pipeline.runner` and `pipeline.steps` normally. The replacement only changes `runner.py`'s previously inline-first two lines, keeps the return statement intact, and the package-relative import fully qualifies the module name at runtime inside `pipeline.runner` (so it does not round-trip through the outer `__init__.py`). Existing behavior is preserved (test cases covered by the visible test file do not depend on the slash addition or import mechanics), and the new requirements are met exactly.",
